## Why This Is Asked
Examines a practical sleep-wake path and basic data-structuring under concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Timer wheel with fixed slots for constant-time operations
- Safe concurrent enqueue and wakeup via spinlocks
- Tick progression and wrap-around handling

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct Thread { int id; struct Thread *next; } Thread;

#define WHEEL_SIZE 64
typedef struct {
  Thread *buckets[WHEEL_SIZE];
  int current_tick;
  SpinLock lock;
} TimerWheel;

void sleep_ms(Thread *t, int ms, TimerWheel *w) {
  int slot = (w->current_tick + ms) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  t->next = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = t;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
}

void timer_tick(TimerWheel *w) {
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  int slot = w->current_tick % WHEEL_SIZE;
  Thread *cur = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = NULL;
  w->current_tick++;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
  while (cur) { wake(cur); cur = cur->next; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for correctness under concurrent enqueues and wakes?
- What changes if ms can exceed wheel size multiple times?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests NUMA-aware page fault design, race-free synchronization, and latency-conscious IO.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA-aware fault handling
- CAS-based per-page race resolution
- per-page state machine and wait queues
- memory barriers and fairness under contention
- optional read-ahead hints

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo
enum PageState { UNCLAIMED, INFLIGHT, FILLED }
volatile int *page_state = ...;
function fault(page) {
  if (CAS(page_state, UNCLAIMED, INFLIGHT)) {
     io_read(page);
     page_state = FILLED;
     wake_waiters(page);
  } else {
     wait(page);
  }
}
```

